<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Germany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bonn Republic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was the common English name for the Federal Republic of Germany between its foundation in 1949 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-reunification">German reunification</a> in 1990. Formed from the American, British and French occupation zones with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> as its capital, it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> and a member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>. Its rapid post-war recovery became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder">Wirtschaftswunder</a>, or economic miracle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-germany.jpg" alt="West Germany" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SKopp, Madden, and other users · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Germany.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalta-conference">Yalta Conference</a> of 4 to 11 February 1945, leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> agreed that post-war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> would be divided into four occupation zones: French in the far west, British in the north-west, American in the south and Soviet in the east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> was divided into four zones as well. The divisions were not meant to dismember Germany. The Federal Republic was founded on 23 May 1949 from twelve states formed in the three western zones, and at first it claimed an exclusive mandate for all of Germany. Three south-western states merged to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> in 1952, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a> joined in 1957.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Federal Republic obtained the full authority of a sovereign state on 5 May 1955 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> conventions, although full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> came only with the Two Plus Four Agreement in 1990. It joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> on 9 May 1955. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer">Konrad Adenauer</a> was chancellor from 1949 to 1963, and he worked for full alignment with NATO and for agreements that grew into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>. Ludwig Erhard and Kurt Georg Kiesinger followed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willy-brandt">Willy Brandt</a> became chancellor in October 1969. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostpolitik">Ostpolitik</a> aimed to improve relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, and in 1970 he knelt at the memorial to the Warsaw Ghetto Uprising. Helmut Schmidt served from 1974 to 1982 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-kohl">Helmut Kohl</a> from 1982.</p><p>Denazification, an Allied programme to remove Nazi personnel and ideology from public life, was unpopular and opposed by Adenauer's government. Laws of 1951 granted amnesties and ended it, and many people with a Nazi past returned to the state apparatus. In 1967 the student Benno Ohnesorg was shot by a policeman during protests against emergency laws.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was a federal parliamentary republic with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>. The president was a largely ceremonial head of state, and the chancellor, elected by the Bundestag, headed the government. The upper house, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundesrat-germany">Bundesrat</a>, represented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-of-germany">Länder</a>. West Berlin was treated as an unofficial eleventh state, though it was under Allied control. After the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> voted to accede to the Federal Republic, and the five reconstituted eastern Länder and a reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> joined on 3 October 1990, raising the number of states from ten to sixteen. The reunited Germany is the direct continuation of West Germany and kept its memberships in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Germany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>West Germany</b> was established as a federal parliamentary republic with a social market economy under the Grundgesetz, the Basic Law of 1949.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Askari who fought in the East African campaign later received pensions from the Weimar Republic and <b>West Germany</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Second World War, American aid, tourism, shipping and rapid urbanisation produced high growth in the 1950s and 1960s, while hundreds of thousands emigrated to <b>West Germany</b> and Australia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…for domestic production expanded industrial output, and companies evaded the embargo through subsidiaries and a clandestine trade network: <b>West Germany</b> remained the largest Western European trading partner until 1973, Japan the chief market outside Africa, Iran supplied oil, Portugal re-exported goods through Mozambique with false certificates of origin,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…million foreign tourists visited Dalmatia in 1965 and 4.75 million in 1970, bringing $275 million, a tenth of federal revenue, by 1969; emigration was legalised in 1963, and by the early 1970s 1.1 million workers, a fifth of the labour force, worked abroad, two thirds in <b>West Germany</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…into self-governing units can be both large and diverse without a central government turning tyrannical, and that dividing power makes it harder for a would-be dictator to seize it all; for this reason the Western Allies required the constitution of <b>West Germany</b>, adopted in 1949, to be federal.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
